record jpegxl-rs.decision.ledger-conventions/1 : decision {
    title "AKR ledger conventions for jpegxl-rs"
    state proposed
    scope [ all ]
    topic ledger-conventions
    decision """
        The AKR ledger at the repository root is the plan-of-record. One namespace,
        jpegxl-rs; structure comes from scope path globs over JPXL/crates/**, not from
        extra namespaces. Kind crosswalk from the retired markdown: roadmap phases and
        milestones become milestone and track; waves and slices become work; HANDOFF
        "Already fixed" becomes decision; HANDOFF "Traps" becomes policy; LESSONS and
        resource limits become decision and constraint; docs/experiments notes become
        observation and evidence with watches; CONFORMANCE becomes milestone acceptance
        plus observation. Generated views under docs/generated are the single reading
        surface; the source markdown is retired to legacy citations. Historical
        milestones are imported with source { kind legacy } so D-028 exempts their old
        evidence commits from the descendant-commit gate.
        """
    context """
        Planning lived in RUST_JXL_ENCODER_ROADMAP.md, JPXL/docs HANDOFF, PLAN and
        CONFORMANCE, and about twenty-five dated docs/experiments notes, all hand
        maintained and drifting. AKR makes the plan typed, checkable and generated.
        """
    consequences """
        Agents propose and revise records and log papercuts instead of editing
        HANDOFF.md; wave commits become ledger writes. Renaming the namespace later
        rewrites every key, so it is fixed here.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.lossy-production-presets/1 : decision {
    title "Balanced and Fast are the production lossy presets; Quality is the exhaustive reference preset"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**",
        path "JPXL/docs/optimize.md"
    ]
    decision """
        Treat RateSearchPreset::Balanced and RateSearchPreset::Fast as the production-intended lossy paths. RateSearchPreset::Quality remains an exhaustive reference/oracle path for comparison, fallback, and research; it is not the primary target for production quality, latency, or efficiency work. New quality passes should first seek bounded, measured improvements to Balanced and Fast without silently routing their normal operation through Quality-tier work.
        """
    context """
        The user clarified this preset contract on 2026-08-19 after Phase Q8 had improved only the Quality tier. Balanced and Fast already carry bounded two-anchor controllers and are the paths intended for real use; Quality deliberately pays exhaustive exact-search cost.
        """
    consequences """
        Quality-only gains may still be recorded, but they do not satisfy a request to improve production encoding. Promotion reports must state which preset changes, prove unchanged presets stay byte-identical where claimed, and measure native Windows behavior when that is the faster available host path.
        """
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.quality-q9-production-default-2026-08-19/1,
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.quality-q9-speed-2026-08-19/1
    ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.mit-only-licensing/1 : decision {
    title "License JPXL under MIT only"
    state proposed
    scope [ all ]
    decision """
        JPXL is licensed under MIT only. Repository license notices, workspace package metadata, SPDX headers, and public documentation must consistently use MIT; the former Apache-2.0 alternative is removed from the project distribution.
        """
    context """
        User-directed licensing choice for publication to LegeApp/JPXL.
        """
    consequences """
        Use LICENSE as the canonical root license and JPXL/LICENSE-MIT as the workspace mirror; do not retain or advertise an Apache-2.0 option.
        """
    source {
        kind internal
        role origin
        path "LICENSE"
        use """
            User-directed project licensing decision.
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.modular-lean-default/1 : decision {
    title "Modular default effort is the lean level 1; the full search is opt-in"
    state active
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/**", path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode/**" ]
    decision """
        Effort::DEFAULT for the lossless modular path is Effort::MIN (level 1): one predictor, no split search, no per-leaf refinement, no palette/squeeze trials, coarse sampling stride, collapsed tree. The full pre-ramp search is retained verbatim at level 7 -- a byte-identical density anchor pinned by the effort_7_budget_is_the_pre_ramp_search test -- and levels 8-9 add finer stride and deeper search for density chasing. Effort is expanded once at the plan_for stage boundary into a ModularSearchBudget (Encoder-plan1.md 12), never re-checked in a kernel.
        """
    context """
        The full search was measured to be chaff on the photo corpus: byte-identical output to level 1 at ~16x the wall time (jpegxl-rs.assessment.modular-effort-lean-default). Quality is preserved unconditionally because every level is exact-lossless.
        """
    consequences """
        Default encodes are 4.6-16.9x faster on photos with byte-identical output (verified: .agent/scratch/effort-ramp-2026-08-10/verify_default_flip.ps1). Flat/paletteable content encodes a few percent larger by default (measured 3.7% on a synthetic screenshot) unless a higher effort is selected. This default-output change is deliberate but validated only on a limited corpus; a broader real-world UI/line-art corpus should confirm the tradeoff before any method is deleted (as opposed to demoted).
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.constraint.correctness-before-speed,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.modular-effort-lean-default ]
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.opt-p-wide-rayon-deps/1 : decision {
    title "wide and rayon are feature-gated default dependencies for Opt-P"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/**" ]
    topic opt-p-wide-rayon-deps
    decision """
        The encoder optimization track may depend on the `wide` (portable SIMD)
        and `rayon` (work-stealing pool) crates. Both are optional Cargo features
        named `simd` and `parallel`, enabled by default on jpxl-core (simd),
        jpxl-encode, jpxl-encode-policy, and jpxl-cli. Builds with
        --no-default-features remain pure-scalar and std::thread-only. Contract A
        holds across thread counts for section emission; SIMD kernels may use
        Contract B float semantics relative to scalar builds and stay decode-valid.
        """
    context """
        Approved during Opt-P leaf-SIMD work after coarse section parallelism landed.
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.optimization-determinism-contract/1 : decision {
    title "Contract A for structural optimization; bounded Contract B for quality policy"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-bitstream/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-entropy/**"
    ]
    decision """
        Default to Contract A for optimization changes: same source and options produce identical codestream bytes across thread counts, scheduling, and scalar versus optimized integer/bitstream paths. Structural refactors (BitWriter bulk path, plan Arc-sharing, coefficient arenas, scratch reuse, Rayon section encoding, integer SIMD) must prove byte-identical output. Contract B permits deliberate quality-policy changes after a recorded corpus gate. Phase 5G's target-rate AQ/quant_lf gate required matched achieved rate, no Butteraugli regression in 12 representative cells, at least 7.5% observed Butteraugli improvement in every cell, RMSE improvement in every cell, and disclosed SSIMULACRA2 regression bounded to 4.5 points. Phase 5I's active-EPF gate separately requires matched achieved rate, no Butteraugli regression in 12 cells, at least 0.6% observed Butteraugli improvement in every cell, RMSE and Butteraugli-pnorm3 improvement in every cell, and any SSIMULACRA2 regression disclosed and bounded to 0.05 points. From the quality track (Phase Q0, 2026-08-17) onward, per jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric, a Contract B change (and any lossless-recoding change that moves target-rate outputs through the rate loop, such as Phase Q0b) is promoted only when, on the standing harness (.agent/scratch/quality-track: three photos x 0.5/1/2 bpp x Fast/Balanced/Quality plus the seven 1024x768 scenes at 1 bpp, candidate versus the previous promoted binary): SSIMULACRA2 mean delta >= 0 and no cell worse than -0.5; Butteraugli mean (max-norm) not worse than +2%; the worst-cell Butteraugli bound is stated on the 3-norm (butteraugli_pnorm3): no cell worse than +5% — the max-norm's single-cell value is reported but not bounded, because Phase Q1's sweeps measured it swinging +8% to +18% in single cells on settings whose SSIMULACRA2, RMSE and 3-norm were all neutral or better (a larger regression is a recorded trade, never an automatic promotion); bytes at matched SSIMULACRA2 not up; Balanced pinned wall within +15% cumulative of the Phase 42 binary (jpxl-p42d), instruction counts reported when the host is loaded; codestream bytes identical across 1 and 4 threads and with AVX2 disabled; djxl and jxl-oxide accept every stream. Future Contract B changes that need different bounds require another explicit revision naming them.
        """
    context """
        VarDCT RD ties are sensitive to tiny float differences. Wide and hand SIMD can change NaN patterns and wrapping. The project already requires software-defined float conversion without fast-math for the reference pair. Phase 5G deliberately changed target-rate quantization policy. Phase 5I deliberately activates decoder restoration by signalling one EPF step and a uniform Sharpness 7 grid; this changes reconstructed pixels without changing coefficient decisions. The Phase 5G/5I bounds predate the SSIMULACRA2-first decision; the quality-track bounds restate the gate in its terms and add the speed budget and the third-party-decoder acceptance that Phase Q0b's jxl-oxide finding showed is not implied by djxl and jpxl-decode agreeing. Phase Q1 moved the worst-cell Butteraugli test to the 3-norm after measuring the max-norm's per-cell noise on the sweep.
        """
    consequences """
        Every structural PR needs scalar-vs-optimized and 1-vs-N-thread byte equality tests where applicable. Float kernel and RD-search work may not claim Contract A without proof. Phase 5 target-rate quality policies may be promoted only while their recorded corpus evidence satisfies the separately stated Butteraugli, RMSE, achieved-rate, pnorm3, and SSIMULACRA2 bounds. Quality-track phases record their harness outputs (ladder/scenes/cjxl TSVs and summary) as evidence and state their speed cost against the +15% cumulative budget so later phases know what remains.
        """
    implements [ @jpegxl-rs.constraint.correctness-before-speed ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-static-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.optimization-ordering/1 : decision {
    title "Optimization order: structure, then parallel, then SIMD"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-bitstream/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-core/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode/**"
    ]
    topic optimization-ordering
    decision """
        Within the encoder optimization pass, implement in this order: (1) separate
        Modular and VarDCT benchmarks plus multiplicity counters; (2) remove repeated
        full-frame work, deep plan clones, and per-varblock allocation; (3) replace
        known-bad algorithms (bit-at-a-time writer, dense DCT32 fallback when factored
        scalar exists, brute-force LZ only if hot); (4) reshape hot scalar loops for
        the compiler; (5) coarse group and section parallelism with a single resource
        policy; (6) explicit SIMD only on remaining leaf kernels; (7) LTO and PGO last.
        Do not start with a general SIMD or Rayon sweep.
        """
    context """
        Advisor static assessment. Aligns with correctness-before-speed (scalar before
        parallelism before SIMD).
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.constraint.correctness-before-speed,
        @jpegxl-rs.constraint.forbid-unsafe-by-default
    ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-static-optimization ]
    source {
        kind legacy
        path "JPXL/optimization-plan.akr"
        excerpt """
            First remove repeated transforms, repeated whole-frame scans, repeated
            emissions, deep coefficient copies, and per-varblock allocations. Then
            parallelize groups. Then vectorize the small number of arithmetic kernels
            still visible at the top of the profile.
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.optimization-pass-opened/1 : decision {
    title "Open the encoder optimization pass under AKR"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/**" ]
    topic optimization-pass-opened
    decision """
        Begin the encoder optimization pass now that M1-M8 exist. PLAN/AGENTS language
        that deferred SIMD and Rayon for the scalar reference phase remains the default
        outside this track; work on this track may introduce coarse parallelism and
        leaf SIMD only after the measurement foundation and structural multiplicity
        fixes land, and only under correctness-before-speed and forbid-unsafe-by-default.
        """
    context """
        VarDCT M1-M8 and Modular density work (slice 19+) are feature-complete enough
        for a performance baseline. PERFORMANCE.md still has no rows. An external advisor
        assessment is captured as jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-static-optimization.
        """
    consequences """
        Optimization work is planned as jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization milestones.
        Density-pin vs cjxl becomes a Modular metric under the foundation milestone, not
        an excuse to skip benches.
        """
    implements [ @jpegxl-rs.constraint.correctness-before-speed ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-static-optimization ]
    source {
        kind legacy
        path "JPXL/optimization-plan.akr"
        excerpt """
            Recommended implementation order: Common foundation, then Modular track,
            then VarDCT track, then parallel and SIMD, finish with LTO and PGO.
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.perceptual-quality-contract/1 : decision {
    title "A minimum SSIMULACRA2 score is the public lossy contract; Fast (70) and Balanced (85) are the production efforts; Quality stays feature-gated until it is smaller at matched score"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-perceptual/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-plan-render/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl/**",
        path "JPXL/tools/**"
    ]
    decision """
        Normal lossy encoding is specified by a minimum perceptual score, not a byte budget: the encoder minimises exact codestream bytes subject to SSIMULACRA2(source, decoded) >= requested score. Two production efforts exist: Fast (web; default --quality 70) and Balanced (archival; default --quality 85). Both accept an explicit --quality 0..100; 100 routes to lossless; the same score must mean the same minimum quality at every effort, and higher effort may only reduce bytes or overshoot, never the achieved score. Hard probe/price budgets are enforced in code for both production efforts with no hidden exhaustive fallback. The exhaustive RateSearchPreset::Quality path and the perceptual Quality effort (policy bank, terminal reducer) are compiled only under the `quality-effort` cargo feature; a public Quality effort is re-exposed only when evidence on the locked holdout shows geomean bytes at matched achieved SSIMULACRA2 <= 0.98x Balanced with Contract B guards intact. --bpp, --bytes and a fixed-quantizer --global-scale remain as expert, benchmarking and constrained-delivery modes; they no longer define the normal lossy policy. Quality-mode policy contains no requested-bitrate branch (at_most_one_bpp and the bpp-keyed chroma QM arms are removed from the quality path); chroma allocation is a per-preset starting value and a policy-bank axis compared at equal score. The production metric is the in-tree jpxl-perceptual implementation (first backend: clean-room SSIMULACRA2, parity-tested); a metric-version change is an encoder-behaviour change.
        """
    context """
        The byte-target contract made the lossy path unusable for real work: a user cannot ask for archival or web quality, only for a byte count whose perceptual meaning changes per image (user, 2026-08-22). The G0 baseline of 2026-08-21 already shows JPXL Balanced 9-12% smaller than cjxl -e7 at equal SSIMULACRA2 on the mid/large photos while trailing Butteraugli 3-norm and PSNR at matched bytes, so the goal is a usable score contract that preserves that byte advantage and the guard metrics, not a libjxl density chase. The registered 2026-08-21 controller plan and the 2026-08-22 metric advice are the outside sources; this decision adopts the controller plan's public contract and effort semantics, keeps the doc's PR 1-5 and PR 7 in scope with PR 6/8 as follow-ups, and records the metric advice as a separate later workstream (JPXL-PQ/JPXL-PCost) in which SSIMULACRA2 and Butteraugli remain independent guards.
        """
    consequences """
        Supersedes jpegxl-rs.decision.lossy-production-presets/1 (Quality as a public exhaustive reference preset). Work is tracked under jpegxl-rs.track.perceptual-quality-controller as PR 0-5 and PR 7 work records with measurable gates; promotion is measured at equal achieved SSIMULACRA2 (geomean byte ratio, BD-rate), never at equal bpp. Rate-mode (--bpp/--bytes) production streams must remain byte-identical through the refactors. The wall-time budget for the perceptual Balanced path is recorded per PR from measurement (provisional PR 4 bound 2.0x rate-mode Balanced at matched score; +25% is the later profiling target), not assumed.
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric/1,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.clean-room-boundary/1,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion/2
    ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.lossy-production-presets/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.libjxl-comparison-2026-08-18/2,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.q5-exhaustive-fallback-is-a-hidden-quality-tier-2026-08-18/3
    ]
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "jpxl-ssimulacra2-quality-controller-plan-2026-08-21"
        use """
            Public quality contract, effort/quality independence, hard budgets, Pareto selection, PR sequence and acceptance gates.
            """
    }
    source {
        kind external
        role rationale
        document "jpxl-encoder-metric-advice-2026-08-22"
        use """
            Metric framework exposes error fields; JPXL-specific metric is a separate later workstream; SSIMULACRA2 and Butteraugli stay independent guards (Goodhart rule).
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.pqc-wall-target-renegotiation-shape/1 : decision {
    title "Renegotiated wall-anchors ceiling (operator-accepted 2026-08-30): derived Balanced ceilings 2.5x (4.3 MP) and 3.5x (12 MP) at 8 threads"
    state active
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**", path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-perceptual/**" ]
    decision """
        Replace the wall-anchors acceptance check's fixed 2.0x constant with the derived ceiling ceiling(anchor, effort) = round_up_0.25(floor_ratio x (1 + margin)), margin = (mean pixel_probes - 1) x per-probe share of the single-probe quality wall. With quiet-host post-E1 inputs (2026-08-30) the Balanced constants are: 2.5x at the 4.3 MP anchor and 3.5x at the 12 MP anchor at 8 threads (3.0x and 4.5x at 4 threads). Measured floors: 1.42x and 2.04x (t8); per-probe shares 0.332 and 0.308; wall-weighted mean canonical probes 3.09. Current HEAD ratios 2.36x and 3.30x already sit under these ceilings. Re-open trigger: any change moving the measured floor by >10% re-derives the ceiling rather than inheriting it.
        """
    context """
        The 2.0x target predates measurement. It is unreachable by exact cuts (rev 9 of the work record), the surrogate route is closed by a thrice-confirmed assessment (S1, S2, S3 kill point), and the RT2 rate-prior route closed at its offline learnability gate on 2026-08-30. Quiet-host inputs (load 1.3-1.8, @jpegxl-rs.evidence.w1b-quiet-host-floors-2026-08-30): 8-thread ratios 2.36x (4.3 MP) and 3.30x (12 MP) at 3 probes with E1-b1/E1-d landed; floors derived by subtracting (probes-1) x (plan+render_metric)/probes from the quality wall. The wall-weighted mean canonical probe count is 3.09 (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.b1-multi-probe-frequency-2026-08-30).
        """
    consequences """
        The check stays open (never weakened unilaterally) until the operator accepts this decision; on acceptance the work record gains a wall-anchors-v2 check with these constants and the 2.0x check is retired to history with the explicit statement that it was set before measurement. The 12 MP floor can only move materially now via a renegotiated memory cap (E1-a retention: -6% wall for ~2.34 GiB peak, measured below the operator's 10% bar on 2026-08-30) or the newly profiled linear_at_depth / XYB leaves.
        """
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.pqc-usable-efforts-cost ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.assessment.surrogate-navigation-does-not-pay-for-wall,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.pqc-wall-attribution-post-s2-2026-08-30
    ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.w1b-quiet-host-floors-2026-08-30 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.quality-miss-fallback-semantics/1 : decision {
    title "An under-target perceptual result is refused at the facade by default; lossless and best-effort are explicit opt-in fallbacks"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quality.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl/**"
    ]
    decision """
        An under-target perceptual result is never an ordinary success at the public facade. When the bounded controller stops at SaturatedTop or UnderTargetWorkCap, the default is to refuse: Encoder returns Error::TargetNotMet carrying a QualityMiss (kind LadderSaturated | WorkBudgetExhausted, requested and best verified scores, probe/price counts, metric version, quality trace), and the CLI exits 1 without creating the output file. Two explicit fallbacks exist via Encoder::with_quality_fallback / --quality-fallback: Lossless emits a mathematically lossless stream reported as PerceptualStatus::FallbackLossless with achieved score 100, and BestEffort emits the finest canonically verified under-target stream under its true status and score. SaturatedFloor remains a success. The policy layer (search_frame_perceptual) still returns every terminal status with bytes; the facade owns the refusal. The rescue probe stays one probe beyond the navigation cap and is documented as an observable per-solve maximum of pixel_probes + 1; folding it inside the cap is a deliberate search-policy change that would require the standing Contract B screen.
        """
    context """
        The 2026-08-24 one-shot advisor memo's first code finding, verified against quality.rs, jpxl/src/lib.rs and jpxl-cli/src/main.rs: the facade wrapped SaturatedTop and UnderTargetWorkCap outcomes as Ok and the CLI wrote the output file and exited 0, so a scripted caller checking only the exit code received a silently under-target stream — incompatible with the hard-floor reading of the perceptual-quality contract. The completed 91-cell locked holdout contains no under-target cells, so refusing changes no canonical-sweep behavior.
        """
    consequences """
        Met, MetAdjacentRungs, MetWorkCap, SaturatedFloor and RescuedFreshStructure outputs stay byte-identical. The public API adds Error::TargetNotMet, QualityMiss, QualityMissKind, QualityFallback, PerceptualStatus::FallbackLossless and Encoder::with_quality_fallback; the CLI adds --quality-fallback lossless|best-effort. A refused encode still appends its jpxl.quality-trace/1 record to JPXL_QUALITY_TRACE so failed searches remain calibration input. Facade, policy-layer and CLI tests now pin the miss path, which previously had no coverage anywhere.
        """
    implements [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.perceptual-quality-contract/1 ]
    author "GitHub Uploader"
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "jpxl-one-shot-quality-controller-memo-2026-08-24"
        use """
            Adopted the memo's PR 0 contract repair: refusal by default, explicit lossless/best-effort fallback modes, structured failure payload, CLI exit-1 with no output file, and public miss-path tests.
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.quant-bias-defaults/1 : decision {
    title "quant_bias defaults are 1 minus x"
