    decision """
        Table L.1 prints the quant_bias defaults as literal expressions (1 - 0.05465 and
        so on), verified against a clean scan after both OCRs collapsed the "1 -" prefix.
        DEFAULT_QUANT_BIAS is approximately [0.9453, 0.9299, 0.9501] in headers/opsin.rs.
        Do not simplify these back to the small constants.
        """
    context """
        Recovered from HANDOFF "Already fixed" (2026-08-02).
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.rate-ladder-interleaves-hfmul-above-the-ceiling/1 : decision {
    title "Above the global_scale ceiling the rate ladder interleaves HfMul with global_scale and couples quant_lf up to 16"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/rate.rs" ]
    topic rate-ladder-upper-segments
    decision """
        Rungs above MAX_GLOBAL_SCALE are no longer whole HfMul multiples at the ceiling. Segment k (k = 2..=65) walks global_scale from floor((k-1)*MAX/k)+1 up to MAX with HfMul = k, so consecutive rungs differ by k units of effective scale (global_scale * HfMul) and segments join within k+1 units; the ladder top stays 65 * MAX. QuantizerChoice::at couples quant_lf to the segment as the largest base * j (j <= k) that stays at or below 16, so the LF/HF balance the ladder holds below the ceiling carries through the first segments; beyond that bound quant_lf stays put. Search paths that recover a quant_lf from a QuantizerChoice must carry the request's base value, never the coupled wire value.
        """
    context """
        Phase Q3's first standing measurement showed the frame-constant HfMul rungs were a rate cliff: HfMul 2 at the ceiling is a full octave finer than HfMul 1, a 65% byte jump on the smooth mid2 photo, so every target between them landed 35% under ('budget spent, not at the ladder's limit'), and large at 2 bpp landed 27% under. The coupling bound of 16 is the product MAX_GLOBAL_SCALE * 16 that the fixed-quantizer defaults have always been allowed to reach; jxl-oxide 0.12.6 narrows LfQuant samples to signed 16 bits (Phase 5F) and a synthetic high-contrast oracle fixture wraps at twice that product.
        """
    consequences """
        Cells whose crossing lies above the ceiling now reach their targets (mid2 2 bpp 698 KB -> 1,080 KB, +2.84 SSIMULACRA2, -31% Butteraugli; large 2 bpp 2.18 MB -> 3.0 MB, +1.6..+2.1 SSIMULACRA2, -18..-22% Butteraugli); every cell below the ceiling keeps its Phase Q2 hash. Balanced still falls back to the exhaustive controller on such cells because the second-anchor guess extrapolates with exponent 2 (about 5.9 s on mid2 at 2 bpp) — a speed follow-up, recorded separately. Requests with quant_lf already at or above 16 see an LF-step sawtooth of at most 1/(k+1) across segment joins above the ceiling.
        """
    implements [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.optimization-determinism-contract/1 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.read-u32-wraps/1 : decision {
    title "read_u32 wraps mod 2^32, it does not error"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-bitstream/**" ]
    topic read-u32-overflow
    decision """
        Per 18181-1 B.2.2, read_u32 computes (offset + v) Umod (1 << 32): it wraps with
        wrapping_add and never returns Overflow on offset + payload. Regression test
        u32_offset_plus_payload_wraps_mod_2_pow_32. Do not harden this back into an error.
        """
    context """
        Recovered from HANDOFF "Already fixed" (2026-08-02). The original scaffold
        returned Overflow; the fix carries a clause citation.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.retire-performance-md/1 : decision {
    title "Retire PERFORMANCE.md as plan-of-record for performance"
    state proposed
    scope [ path ".agent/scratch/**", path "JPXL/docs/**" ]
    topic performance-docs-authority
    decision """
        JPXL/docs/PERFORMANCE.md is no longer the plan-of-record for performance
        baselines, measurement rules, or profiling results. Authority is split:

        - Durable claims (baseline rules, bench entry points, summary observations,
          decisions): AKR records under jpegxl-rs.policy.performance-baseline-rules,
          jpegxl-rs.policy.jpxl-bench-entry-points, and jpegxl-rs.observation.preopt-*
          (and successors).
        - Full raw run logs, flamegraph artifacts, HOTSPOTS/PROVENANCE: .agent/scratch/
          (not pruned by akr build). Current ladder:
          .agent/scratch/preopt-baseline-2026-08-06.md; current flamegraphs:
          .agent/scratch/flamegraphs/preopt-2026-08-06/.

        PERFORMANCE.md remains only as a short stub pointing at those AKR keys and
        scratch paths. Do not re-accumulate tables or rules in the markdown file.
        Frozen experiment writeups may still go in JPXL/docs/experiments/ when an
        experiment report is warranted.
        """
    context """
        PERFORMANCE.md duplicated material that already lived as AKR observations and
        was drifting from scratch logs. AGENTS.md living-docs discipline prefers small
        generated views over hand-maintained performance tables.
        """
    consequences """
        Opt-F acceptance and work language that required PERFORMANCE.md baseline rows
        must be revised to AKR observations + baseline-rules policy. Agents update
        observations and scratch, not long tables in PERFORMANCE.md.
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.s3-coefficient-domain-decimated-surrogate/1 : decision {
    title "S3: coefficient-domain decimated reconstruction, gated on engaged-cell wall ≤ 0.980"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-core/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-perceptual/**",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-plan-render/**"
    ]
    decision """
        Build the coefficient-domain decimated surrogate reconstruction: the 2:1 box average of a length-N normative IDCT computed exactly as a length-N/2 normative IDCT of folded coefficients (Y_0=X_0; Y_j=cos(pi j/2N)X_j - sin(pi j/2N)X_{N-j}; X_{N/2} annihilates), per axis, with a bit-exact low-pass fast branch when the partner region is +0.0, hoisted to the quantized arrays so dequant/CfL skip 3/4 of cells. Phased with pre-registered kill points: K0 economics model reproduction (done, 0.34% agreement); K1 measured varblock-stage survival alpha must be <= 0.30 before integration; K2 end-to-end c_rm within 0.02 of projection; K3 dev estimate error at t90 <= 0.8; K4 default-enable of surrogate-navigation only at engaged-cell wall geomean <= 0.980 pooled over both holdouts with floor 0/0, bytes geomean <= 1.0005 (worst <= 1.04), engaged p90 <= 1.18, mispropose <= 20% bounded to one correction, and 20/20 default-path byte identity.
        """
    context """
        S2 closed with surrogate navigation wall-neutral at 0.398x render+metric cost. Phase-0 paired estimator over the S2 holdout artifacts (validated 0.34% against the observed A/B) shows: the pixel plan floors effective probe cost at 0.154x; half-res gaborish/EPF/colour+metric floor c_rm at 0.2005 giving c_rm(alpha)=0.198*alpha+0.2005; engaged-cell wall can never beat 0.961; break-even c_rm 0.305; wall <= 0.98 requires alpha <= 0.255. A 0.97 gate is physically unreachable (alpha <= 0.12). Corpus-wide payoff even on full success is ~0.2-0.4% (11.4% engagement). The phase is ordered so the cheapest disconfirming measurement comes first; a negative K1 closes surrogate navigation permanently with the sharper statement that it cannot be made to pay by making the surrogate cheaper.
        """
    consequences """
        New kernels in jpxl-core (dct.rs fold + half IDCT, varblock.rs half_samples_from_coefficients_into with box-average fallback for never-emitted special transforms); the render decimate branch writes half-res planes directly and deletes decimate_rows; evaluator recycles the half planes (full-res capacity preserved) to keep the canonical render allocation-free; sigma stays full-res + 2x2 average verbatim so only one observation change enters the recalibration; full S2-style recalibration (fresh shadow corpus, k refit, floor re-derivation on dev, both holdout A/Bs, mispropose sweep) before any default-enable.
        """
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.multifidelity-quality-search ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.surrogate-navigation-does-not-pay-for-wall ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric/1 : decision {
    title "Optimize for SSIMULACRA2 first; butteraugli becomes a secondary axis to refine later"
    state active
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**", path "test-set/**" ]
    decision """
        SSIMULACRA2 is the primary metric for promoting a lossy encoder policy. Butteraugli is retained as a reported secondary axis and as the eventual refinement target, but it no longer holds a veto.

        Concretely, replacing the previously implicit Contract B tie-break ('uniformly non-regressing Butteraugli without material SSIMULACRA2 loss'):

        - A candidate is promotable when SSIMULACRA2 does not regress materially on any screened cell and improves on the corpus as a whole. Butteraugli is reported per cell and pooled, and a butteraugli regression is a recorded caveat rather than a blocker.
        - Both metrics are still measured and both still appear in every screen table. This changes which one decides, not what is collected.
        - The rule is stated BEFORE each screen runs, per the process note Phase 7.0 raised.
        """
    context """
        Phase 7.0 demonstrated that a single lever can move the two metrics in opposite directions reliably and at scale: a rate-aware quantizer improved butteraugli in 20 of 28 cells (mean -5.21%, best -29.9%) while regressing SSIMULACRA2 in 24 of 28 (mean -1.80), at properly matched rate. With no stated tie-break, the promotion decision would have been made by whichever metric happened to be quoted first.

        The two metrics are not interchangeable. Butteraugli aggregates by max-norm and rewards reduced peak error, so it favours smoothing; SSIMULACRA2 is structure- and detail-sensitive and penalises lost texture. Phase 7.0's over-zeroing is exactly that divergence.

        User decision, 2026-08-12: optimize for SSIMULACRA2 and refine for perceptual later.
        """
    consequences """
        IMMEDIATE. Phase 6.5's quantizer-donated frequency weight becomes promotable and is promoted. It was rejected under the old rule solely because butteraugli was not uniformly non-regressing. Re-read with SSIMULACRA2 primary it improves 14 of 14 scenes at 0.5 and 1 bpp (mean +4.85 and +0.70), is neutral at 2 and 4 bpp (mean -0.00 and -0.07, worst single cell -0.18), and butteraugli is ALSO net positive pooled (better in 17 of 28, mean -0.17%) -- so it is not even a trade.

        Phase 7.0's rate-distortion quantizer stays rejected, now for the primary reason rather than a secondary one.

        HISTORICAL PROMOTIONS REMAIN VALID, with one caveat to revisit. Phase 5G (AQ Off, quant_lf 8, no LF fill) was promoted on butteraugli but also improved SSIMULACRA2 in 10 of its 12 cells; the exceptions are small3/1 (-0.37) and small4/1 (-4.37). That small4/1 loss is material under the new rule and should be re-examined when the corpus next gets attention -- it is recorded here rather than silently inherited. Phase 5I (active EPF, uniform Sharpness 7) improved SSIMULACRA2 in 11 of 12 cells with a single -0.0351 exception and is unaffected.

        WHAT THIS DOES NOT MEAN. Butteraugli is not abandoned. The measured corpus gap against cjxl effort-7 is still stated in butteraugli terms, and closing it remains the track's goal; this decision orders the work, putting a metric the encoder can currently move in a consistent direction first. When the track returns to butteraugli it will do so with the structural deficits (rate proxy, quantizer) already addressed, which is the condition Phase 7.0 showed was missing.
        """
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.donor-weight-wins-at-low-rate-and-fades-2026-08-12,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.the-quantizer-is-the-lever-and-its-rate-proxy-is-the-defect-2026-08-12
    ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5-quality-screen-2026-08-12,
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-quality-screen-2026-08-12
    ]
}

